newIdeas.revised_mechanism:
Training-free and run at keyframes only. Point3R is the main model; StreamVGGT is the second model, with each cached token given a 3D anchor from its frame's predicted pointmap. CUT3R/TTT3R is measured only, as the latent reference.

(a) Evidence. Each entry whose anchor projects into the current frustum, using the predicted pose, is compared with the predicted depth at that pixel.
- If predicted depth exceeds the entry's depth by more than max(tau_abs, tau_rel*d) and decoder confidence is high, the entry is in observed free space and gets log-odds +w(conf).
- If it lies on the observed surface (within the margin), it gets -w.
- If it is behind the surface, it is occluded and unchanged.
- Log-odds are clamped, OctoMap/ReFusion-style; credit Keller 2013 and ReFusion as the source of the filter. Margins are scaled by a per-frame pose-uncertainty proxy (Point3R confidence, or the disagreement between consecutive pose predictions) to address the reviewer's pose-noise concern.

(b) Action. Above the threshold, the entry is quarantined: masked from cross-attention but kept, and restored if later views support it again. A hard-delete variant is also run. Quarantine is the default because it also protects against false positives from pose error.

(c) Diagnostic that makes the persistence-of-importance claim testable. For entries inside GT changed volumes, log the attention mass they receive from B frames before and after the change becomes visible. If that mass does not drop, attention-frequency and importance criteria cannot evict them by construction (H2 mechanism).

Baselines at equal memory size:
- carry (no forgetting);
- reset at the start of B (LongStream-style);
- time (age cap or FIFO);
- frequency (H2O / Evict3R-style attention counts);
- query-similarity (InfiniteVGGT-style);
- importance (confidence);
- geometry score (GHOST, if code exists);
- Ray-Aware retain-or-replace (2605.05749, if code exists);
- write-side suppression (RayMap3R-style masking of changed-region writes), to show that blocking writes does not remove old ghosts;
- GT-oracle deletion (upper bound).
Drop MemoryVLA-style merging; it is a redundancy operator and is already covered by Point3R's own fusion.

newIdeas.revised_first_experiment:
About 6 GPU-hours plus 1.5 weeks of engineering. This is the H1 oracle gate before any operator work.
- Week 1:
  - Hook Point3R's pointer positions, features, and per-layer cross-attention to pointers.
  - Pick 20 3RScan reference/rescan pairs with at least 3 annotated changed objects, plus 5 pairs with no annotated change as a control.
  - Choose B's first frame by GT co-visibility with A's last keyframe.
  - Project 3RScan instance and change masks into B frames with GT poses.
  - Sanity-check that Point3R registers B into A's frame (report the failure rate). If more than 30% fail, switch the main set to ReplicaCAD rearrangement renders.
- Week 2: run 4 conditions:
  - reset at B;
  - carry;
  - carry + GT-oracle quarantine of pointers inside the old volumes of moved or removed objects;
  - carry + oracle quarantine of an equal number of random static pointers (controls for memory-size effects).
- Report:
  - AbsRel and delta<1.25 on B, separately on changed-object pixels, static pixels, and pixels whose rays pass through vacated volumes;
  - B pose ATE/RPE in A's frame;
  - ghost rate;
  - attention mass on stale pointers over B.
  Paired bootstrap over pairs.
- GO to operator work (contradiction quarantine vs frequency, similarity and importance eviction) only if all hold:
  - oracle beats carry by at least 10% relative on changed or vacated-ray pixels, with a CI excluding 0;
  - static pixels stay within 2%;
  - random-quarantine of equal size does not give the same gain.

original.relation_to_shortlist:
- #2 and #7: these keep factual memory of one scene for its revisits (snapshots, a per-scene LoRA, a scene-coordinate head). This card keeps experiential memory across different scenes for use on new scenes.
- #11: this is the 3D, experiential counterpart of #11's folding of LM session states into a persistent initial state. #11's ridge fold can serve as a gradient-free formation baseline.
- #16: the replay arm (M5) could absorb #16 as Arc 1's continual-learning chapter.
Prior work found in one search:
- CUT3R's learnable start tokens;
- OnlineX 2603.02134 (the start state as a trained generic prior, not continual);
- Human3R 2510.06219 (VPT on a frozen CUT3R);
- Scal3R (about 1% trainable tokens);
- Test3R 2506.13750 (test-time prompt tuning for DUSt3R);
- prompt methods for continual test-time adaptation (2412.09308; CVPR 2024 'Each Test Image Deserves A Specific Prompt').
None of these keeps a continual pool of start states across scenes.

It fills the missing cell of Arc 2 (life cycle of a fact):
- #9 decides when to buy a fact into the weights;
- #3 deletes the store copy after consolidation;
- #1 handles revisions inside the weights.

None of them measures whether the store can still correct the weights after a fact changes. This card adds:
- a coherence term for #3's eviction gate: once the text is gone, nothing can override a stale copy in the weights;
- a measured expiry cost for #9;
- #1's AlphaEdit setup reused as the write-through route. P4 cuts the writes that create #1's garbage.

It reuses Arc 2's models and data. It differs from the dropped idea I12 (exact deletion inside a text-only memory) and from Agentic Unlearning (joint deletion, with no value updates, no dose-response and no write-time predictor).

A 3D echo, not a new card: the CUT3R states banked in #2 are a latent copy of a scene. The same SOR measurement applies when a scene that has changed is warm-started from a stale snapshot, which is #2's 'release changed regions' step.

original.kill_criterion:
Kill if all three hold:
- rule operations keep under 40% of Mem-alpha's gain;
- the extractive overwrite keeps under 30% of MemAgent's gain;
- the RL policies degrade no more than the rule under the hazard and length shifts (a difference of 2 points or less).

That would mean RL learned a decision policy that no cheap rule reproduces, which supports Section 7.3. The project would then pivot to interpreting which features the RL policy uses.

Also stop if Memalpha-4B's card does not tie it to Mem-alpha, or if its tool format cannot be prefix-forced.

original.relation_to_shortlist:
New. It is the external-store twin of #5, testing the same claim outside the model, and belongs to Arc 3 (multi-timescale consolidation theory).

It differs from:
- #6: old experiences displaced by new ones under a k-slot cap, using a quota per collision group rather than a temporal one;
- #10: overwrite decisions inside a single slot;
- the dropped idea I41: an offline policy deciding what earns an always-in-context slot. Here the core block is only one tier under test, not a method.

If H1 holds, it gives Arc 4 a timing-only manager with two parameters.
